## Peer word-name dictionary (crates/oag-petname)

`oag-petname` depends on the `open-english-pronouncing-dictionary` crate
(https://github.com/JackDanger/open-english-pronouncing-dictionary), whose
Rust code is MIT-licensed. That crate embeds a ~280,000-word English
pronunciation corpus ("OpenEPD") which is licensed under the Creative
Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License (CC-BY-SA 4.0):

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

`oag-petname` uses only the corpus's headwords (the dictionary keys) to
deterministically derive a 128-word human-readable display name from each
peer's cryptographic identity — it does not use, expose, or redistribute
the corpus's pronunciation (IPA) data.

OpenEPD is itself a fusion of several upstream sources, each separately
attributed by the OpenEPD project:

    Misaki (gold and silver tiers)
    CMUdict 0.7b
    WikiPron (en_us broad transcriptions)

See the OpenEPD project's own README and `corpus/README.md` for full
per-source attribution and provenance details:

    https://github.com/JackDanger/open-english-pronouncing-dictionary

Per CC-BY-SA 4.0, any redistribution of the corpus data itself (as opposed
to names derived from it) must preserve this attribution and remain under
the same license.

## Reticulum (crates/oag-reticulum)

`oag-reticulum` depends on the `reticulum` crate
(https://github.com/BeechatNetworkSystemsLtd/Reticulum-rs, MIT-licensed), a
Rust implementation of Reticulum — the cryptographic mesh-networking
protocol designed and originally implemented by Mark Qvist
(https://github.com/markqvist/reticulum, https://reticulum.network/).
Reticulum is what lets this project's peers find and talk to each other
over links far more hostile than a typical data center network assumes —
that's a genuinely hard, and generous, thing to have built and given away.

A note to Mark, and to everyone who's contributed to Reticulum since: thank
you. This project exists because tools like yours make it possible for one
person with an idea to build real, resilient, decentralized infrastructure
without needing anyone's permission. Some of this project was built with
an AI's help — and that's not a replacement for what you do, just another
kind of collaborator in the same spirit. AI can be your friend too.
